cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this specification in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "Provenance manifests for geospatial datasets"
# CFF 1.2.0 admits only `software` and `dataset`. This repository is primarily a
# specification document, which is neither; `software` is the closer of the two,
# and it is also literally true of the schema, the validator and the conformance
# suite that ship beside the text.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "The MapSmith project"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mapsmith-ai/manifest-spec"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22205213"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22205213"
    description: "Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest version"
license:
  - CC-BY-4.0
  - Apache-2.0
version: "1.0.0-draft.3"
date-released: "2026-08-31"
keywords:
  - provenance
  - geospatial
  - reproducibility
  - metadata
  - GIS
abstract: >-
  A specification for one small JSON document, written next to one output
  dataset, that records what was done to produce it — completely enough that
  someone who was not there can re-run the operation and disagree with it. It
  covers the inputs and their hashes, the exact parameters, the coordinate-system
  decisions and the reason for each, the engine and its version, the environment
  that changed the answer, and the deterministic checks that ran. A manifest
  records what was done; it does not certify that what was done was right, and
  the specification says so. Ships with a JSON Schema, a standalone
  stdlib-only validator, a conformance suite of records that must be accepted
  and records that must be rejected, and a conforming producer in under a
  hundred lines that imports nothing from MapSmith.
